Use one shared Git remote for both machines, then use SSH/SFTP for the VM and FileZilla to verify the final files.

Best practice:
- Host = source machine
- VM = test/deploy machine
- Shared repo = GitHub/GitLab or a bare Git repo on the host
- Git handles synchronization
- FileZilla validates the actual files on the VM

1) Install required tools on Ubuntu
On both the host and VM:

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y git openssh-client filezilla
```

2) Set up SSH access from host to VM
On host:

```bash
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "host-to-vm"
cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
```

Copy the public key to the VM:

```bash
ssh-copy-id user@VM_IP
```

Test:

```bash
ssh user@VM_IP
```

3) Option A: Shared repository via GitHub/GitLab (recommended)
This is the cleanest way.

On host:
```bash
mkdir -p ~/projects
cd ~/projects
git clone git@github.com:YOUR_USER/YOUR_REPO.git
cd YOUR_REPO
git checkout -b dev
```

On VM:
```bash
ssh user@VM_IP
mkdir -p ~/projects
cd ~/projects
git clone git@github.com:YOUR_USER/YOUR_REPO.git
cd YOUR_REPO
git checkout -b dev
```

Now both machines use the same remote repo. Sync changes like this:

Host:
```bash
cd ~/projects/YOUR_REPO
# edit files
git status
git add .
git commit -m "update from host"
git push origin dev
```

VM:
```bash
cd ~/projects/YOUR_REPO
git pull origin dev
```

To push from VM:
```bash
git add .
git commit -m "update from VM"
git push origin dev
```

4) Option B: No external Git remote: use a bare repo on the host
If you do not want GitHub/GitLab, create a bare repo on the host and use it as the central repository.

On host:
```bash
mkdir -p ~/git-repos
cd ~/git-repos
git init --bare YOUR_REPO.git
```

Clone to host working copy:
```bash
mkdir -p ~/projects
cd ~/projects
git clone /home/YOUR_USER/git-repos/YOUR_REPO.git
cd YOUR_REPO
git config user.name "Your Name"
git config user.email "you@example.com"
echo "# My Project" > README.md
git add README.md
git commit -m "Initial commit"
git push origin master
```

Clone the same repo on the VM from the host over SSH:
```bash
ssh user@VM_IP
mkdir -p ~/projects
cd ~/projects
git clone ssh://user@HOST_IP/home/YOUR_USER/git-repos/YOUR_REPO.git
cd YOUR_REPO
```

Then sync:
```bash
# On host
cd ~/projects/YOUR_REPO
# modify files
git add .
git commit -m "changes from host"
git push origin master
```

```bash
# On VM
cd ~/projects/YOUR_REPO
git pull origin master
```

5) Keep both machines in sync
Typical workflow:

Host:
```bash
cd ~/projects/YOUR_REPO
git pull origin master
# make changes
git add .
git commit -m "host changes"
git push origin master
```

VM:
```bash
cd ~/projects/YOUR_REPO
git pull origin master
# make changes
git add .
git commit -m "vm changes"
git push origin master
```

If you want to avoid conflicts:
```bash
git fetch origin
git status
git checkout main
git pull origin main
```

6) Validate with FileZilla
After syncing to the VM, connect to the VM using SFTP in FileZilla:

- Host: VM_IP
- Protocol: SFTP - SSH File Transfer Protocol
- Logon Type: Normal
- User: your_vm_user
- Password: your_vm_password
- Port: 22

Then browse to the project directory, for example:
```bash
/home/your_user/projects/YOUR_REPO
```

Use FileZilla to:
- Compare local and remote directory trees
- Check that updated files exist
- Confirm file timestamps changed
- Verify no missing or stale files
- Use “View > Directory Comparison” if needed

7) Quick end-to-end example
From host:
```bash
cd ~/projects/YOUR_REPO
echo "update" >> notes.txt
git add notes.txt
git commit -m "update notes"
git push origin master
```

From VM:
```bash
ssh user@VM_IP
cd ~/projects/YOUR_REPO
git pull origin master
ls -l
```

Then in FileZilla, reconnect to the VM and verify the same files are present in the project folder.

